t=table();k=pd.read_csv(root/'revision/results/corrected/ibge2018/kitagawa_endpoints.csv');k24=pd.read_csv(root/'revision/results/corrected/ibge2024/kitagawa_endpoints.csv');m24=pd.read_csv(root/'revision/results/corrected/ibge2024/count_models.csv');n=m24.query('series=="age_adjusted_ge7" and model=="negbin"').iloc[0]
blocks=[
('1. Epidemiological interpretation',f"The national domain of completed ages ≥7 is explicit in the abstract, methods, results, Table 1, figures and discussion. The corrected Brazil-2014-standardised endpoint change is {t.loc['brazil2014_ge7','relative_change']:.2f}%, while the slope over all eight years is {t.loc['brazil2014_ge7','slope']:.5f} per 100,000/year (95% CI {t.loc['brazil2014_ge7','ci_low']:.5f} to {t.loc['brazil2014_ge7','ci_high']:.5f}). We do not interpret non-significance as stability. The negative-binomial age-adjusted model with IBGE 2024 remains positive ({n.annual_pct_change:.2f}% annually, 95% CI {n.ci_low:.2f} to {n.ci_high:.2f}; p = {n.p_value:.3f}); it is reported alongside all six national specifications, not selected as the preferred model.",'Abstract; Methods: domains and trends; Results: National domain; Discussion; Table 1; S4'),
('2. Age standardisation','We added the direct-standardisation equation and weights summing to one within each analysed domain. Brazil-2014 weights are fixed across years and independently normalised within ≥7, 5–34 and each other domain; each denominator revision uses its corresponding 2014 population. Ages ≥90 form one matching open interval. WHO calculations aggregate five-year rates, combine the three ≥90 weights, and assign three fifths of the 5–9 weight to 7–9 before renormalisation. The latter is explicitly described as a uniform-within-band approximation. Independent calculations checked every rate and weight sum; Brazil-standardised and aligned crude rates agree in the standard year.','Methods: Denominators and direct standardisation; S5; standard_weights.csv; independent_output_checks.json'),
('3. Age definitions and denominators','Table 1 now includes the aligned crude ≥7 series with slope, interval and absolute/relative endpoint changes. The principal uses completed calendar age, with the 29-February anniversary on 1 March in non-leap years. Invalid dates and birth after death are excluded; no upper-age exclusion is added. Calendar age changed 23 candidate single-year bins (23 retained), without changing membership in the five named domains; the record-by-record audit is provided. The historical continuous >6 construction stays separate and reproducible. The corrected 2021 aligned crude rate differs from the original PDF because of U071; the original 1.258 is not imposed on the revision.','Methods: Calendar ages; Table 1; S1–S2; record_selection_and_age_transitions.csv'),
('4. Kitagawa decomposition',f"We added Kitagawa's original 1955 reference (doi:10.1080/01621459.1955.10501299), both symmetric equations and the exact identity. All six endpoint pairs were repeated with IBGE 2024. The 2014–2021 composition share is {k.iloc[0].composition_percent:.2f}% under IBGE 2018 and {k24.iloc[0].composition_percent:.2f}% under IBGE 2024. Contributions over 100% mean a negative rate component offsets composition. Unrounded identities pass at 1e−12; each displayed term is rounded independently, explaining last-digit discrepancies. Age-level contributions are supplied and the interpretation remains non-causal.",'Methods: Kitagawa; Results: Composition; Figure 2; S6; kitagawa_age_contributions.csv'),
('5. Mortality at ages 5–34',f"The abstract now highlights standardisation within 5–34. Corrected crude mortality increases by {t.loc['crude_age_5_34','absolute_change']:.4f} per 100,000 ({t.loc['crude_age_5_34','relative_change']:.2f}%); the Brazil-standardised increase is {t.loc['brazil2014_age_5_34','absolute_change']:.4f} ({t.loc['brazil2014_age_5_34','relative_change']:.2f}%). These replace the approximate 0.037 from the reference rather than retaining it after selection changes. Removing 2020 gives a positive within-group-standardised slope (p = {table(scenario='without_2020').loc['brazil2014_age_5_34','p_value']:.3f}). For 2014–2019 the slope is positive but its interval includes zero (p = {table(scenario='prepandemic').loc['brazil2014_age_5_34','p_value']:.3f}); we therefore removed the assertion that every sensitivity establishes a significant trend. The actual years remain the covariate. The same analyses for 7–34, both denominator revisions, Brazil and WHO standards, and three count families are provided. We do not claim a historical reversal or change point.",'Abstract; Results: 5–34 and sensitivities; Discussion; S5, S7; Figure S1'),
('6. Record selection and statistical models','Historical exclusions are separated into 56 COVID-related records and one reduced-key removal. Adding U071 excludes one additional 2021 event; the overlap audit shows 18 U071 records in 2020 already excluded and 18 of 19 in 2021 already excluded by the original rule. The principal retains both ambiguous 2018 rows: all public fields were inspected, but occupation and seven administrative fields differ and the death-declaration number is unavailable. Matching dates/causes cannot establish identity. Historical-key collapse and retention of all COVID mentions are explicit one-condition sensitivities. The nine age bands, log-link equations, population offset and reference band are specified. Quasi-Poisson intervals use residual-df t critical values; Poisson and negative binomial use normal Wald intervals. Mean-model convergence, dispersion, theta, warnings and errors are retained. Negative-binomial theta iteration-limit warnings are disclosed rather than described as exact Poisson equivalence or omitted. Independent matrix/IRLS calculations checked all 252 count models.','Methods: Selection and models; S1, S4, S8; duplicate_pair_audit.md; independent_model_checks.csv'),
]
